% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 6
% composition-elements: 6/4 metre heard two ways, as two dotted-half beats (3+3) and as three half-note beats (2+2+2); two layers crossing three against two, the melody in one grouping and the ostinato in the other, swapping in the middle section; I-IV-V harmony (G, C, D); named intervals (G-B major third, D-A perfect fourth, the ostinato's G-D perfect fifths); ABA' form
% composition-listen: Thomas Mapfumo and the Blacks Unlimited — "Shumba" (1981), on the compilation Shumba: Vital Hits of Zimbabwe (Earthworks, 1990)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thomas+Mapfumo+Shumba+Blacks+Unlimited
% composition-bridge: Hear electric guitar and bass take interlocking cyclical roles associated with mbira-informed chimurenga sound.
% composition-context: Thomas Mapfumo's chimurenga music connects Shona musical resources with anticolonial and post-independence political expression. This new 6/4 exercise is not chimurenga.
% composition-distance: Original pitches, harmony and rhythmic cells; no mbira-derived guitar line, lyric or arrangement from the recording is copied.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"5-8","role":"clapping group over the two layers","prompt":"Which layer do you clap?","choices":["the melody's 2+2+2","the ostinato's 3+3","only beat one"],"replyBars":4}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"11-12","role":"bells or recorder soloist","prompt":"Add one note above the last G","choices":["major third (B)","perfect fourth (C)","perfect fifth (D)"],"replyBars":1}
% dossier-function: original instrumental layering piece for Grade 6: a melody group and an ostinato group play in different groupings of the same six beats, swap groupings in the middle, and a clapping group and a soloist make choices over them
% dossier-elements: twelve bars in 6/4 (six quarter notes); G major, melody pitch set D-E-F#-G-A-B-C-D-E, range D4-E5; ostinato G3-A4 on chord roots and fifths; A (bars 1-4) melody in 3+3 (three quarters then a half and a quarter, or a half, quarter and dotted half) over an ostinato in three half notes; B (bars 5-8) melody in three half notes over an ostinato in two dotted halves, so the crossing swaps; A' (bars 9-12) returns with a new turn in bar 11 and the cadence on a dotted-half G; harmony G-C-D-G, C-G-C-D, G-C-D-G (I-IV-V); ABA' form
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 132 is the target; begin at 100. Rehearsal key G major; the melody's written range D4-E5 suits soprano recorder, xylophone or glockenspiel (and lies inside the young-unison C4-D5 range except two E5s, for voices on "loo"); the ostinato G3-A4 suits bass xylophone, low bells, cello or keyboard. Teach in steps: count six quarters and clap 1 and 4 (3+3), then 1, 3 and 5 (2+2+2); half the class claps each at once; learn the melody of A and B; learn the ostinato; put the layers together; swap groups; add the clapping choice in B and the soloist's interval on the last chord.
% dossier-accompaniment: The ostinato (second voice) and the G-C-D chords are newly authored. The page's folk band was chosen as a plain, non-specific accompaniment; it is not chimurenga, not mbira and must not be labelled Zimbabwean. The class's two layers carry the crossing; mute the band's drums if they blur the 2+2+2 against 3+3. Do not imitate a guitar or mbira part from the recording.
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental piece for recorders, barred percussion, keyboard and body percussion; no words, so that the lesson stays on the two groupings rather than on invented text in place of Mapfumo's Shona lyrics. Any words added later should be recorded as a new adaptation.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed range, metre and teaching constraints. Not traditional, not collected from any community, not a chimurenga song or an mbira part, and not modelled on the reference's melody, guitar lines or arrangement; the Thomas Mapfumo recording is a listening bridge only.
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for the melody, ostinato, chords and this ABC encoding. "Shumba" and its recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included.
% dossier-respect: Name Thomas Mapfumo as a Zimbabwean singer and bandleader, born in 1945, whose chimurenga ("struggle") music brought Shona mbira patterns and Shona-language songs to the electric band during the liberation war and after independence in 1980, and who later lived in exile in the United States; name the Blacks Unlimited's guitarists. Teach the word chimurenga, the political history and Shona sources; do not reduce the genre to a guitar texture, and never present this classroom piece as chimurenga.
% dossier-review: Grade 6 classroom music teacher, Zimbabwean music educator or culture bearer, second score reader and student playing trial; all reviews still required
