N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; the draft's opening pickup and the one-eighth "pickup" bar in the middle were removed by rewriting. Bar-by-bar audit, every bar six quarters: melody bar 1 B d B quarters, A half, G quarter; bar 2 E G C5 quarters, G half, E quarter; bar 3 F# A D5 quarters, C5 half, A quarter; bar 4 B half, A quarter, G dotted half; bars 5-8 three half notes each (E5 C5 G; D5 B G; C5 E G; A F# D); bar 9 = bar 1, bar 10 = bar 2; bar 11 F# A D5 quarters, A half, F# quarter; bar 12 = bar 4. Ostinato: three half notes (root, fifth, root) in bars 1-4 and 9-12, two dotted halves (root, fifth) in bars 5-8, ending on G3 dotted halves. Melody pitch set D-E-F#-G-A-B-C-D-E, range D4-E5, largest leaps the major sixths at the section joins (G4 up to E5 into bar 5, D4 up to B4 into bar 9) and the minor sixth C5-E4 inside bar 7, with thirds, fourths and fifths elsewhere; ostinato G3-A4. The analyzer reads the half notes that cross the 3+3 grouping as syncopation; that is the intended hemiola, not a syn-co-pa figure. Form ABA'. Originality check: the B-D-B-A-G opening and the half-note arpeggios were compared with what the reference's guitar and vocal lines expose and with common children's songs; no shared figure was found; triad arpeggios and a three-against-two layering are common property.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 6 players hold their own grouping while the other layer crosses it at quarter = 132, and how the page's folk band plays 6/4, can only be settled by a classroom trial. The original release of "Shumba" (1981) is taken from Banning Eyre's track notes and the 1990 Earthworks compilation from discographies; both need checking against an authorized source. A Zimbabwean music educator should judge whether the mbira-guitar comparison is accurate and respectful.
